// Decides which prospects go to the domain-editor lookup. Two cases qualify, and the second is the
// one that is easy to miss.
//
// 1. No address at all. The obvious case: the page named nobody, the contact page had nothing, and
//    the name-based finder needs a name it does not have.
//
// 2. A role inbox. The page gave up sell@, info@, support@ or tips@ - an address, so the ladder
//    above stops - but pitching a shared inbox is close to worthless, and the whole point of this
//    workflow is to reach the person who writes the lists. Measured on 29 real authors: 8 of the 14
//    addresses a finder returned were exactly this upgrade, sell@ to a named person, info@ to a
//    named person. Treating "we have an address" as "we are done" throws all of those away.
//
// A verified personal address is never touched: it is already the thing the lookup is trying to
// find, and replacing it would cost money to get a worse answer.
